{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2341 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "f79d0a85908f777fa5e0adced0e4bbdc0c16be749349861fbbc2cbf7760085e6" :edb/db-before-t 2340 :edb/db-after-t 2341 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535653 50 #inst "2026-10-09T04:31:31.255Z" 13194139535653 true] [17592186166680 1020 #edb/ref 17592186359369 13194139535653 true] [17592186166682 1020 #edb/ref 17592186359362 13194139535653 true] [17592186167263 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535653 false] [17592186167263 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535653 true] [17592186167263 1100 "Let $f(x, y) = x^{2} - 4y$, where $x = 2st$ and $y = s^{2}t$. Calculate $\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial s}$." 13194139535653 false] [17592186167263 1100 "Let $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-4y,$ where $x=2st$ and $y={s}^{2}t.$ Calculate $\\frac{∂f}{∂s}.$" 13194139535653 true] [17592186167263 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069068/assets/21cec766fe72965697304ef4288ffb2b090ddfca179210165dbcfd42ec490b27.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t)$ and $y=y(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂s}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂s}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =2(2st)=4st, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $s,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂s} & =2t, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂s} & =2st.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂s} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂s} \\\\  & =(4st)(2t)+(-4)(2st) \\\\  & =8s{t}^{2}-8st \\\\  & =8st(t-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535653 true] [17592186167263 1145 "Keep $t$ fixed. The chain rule gives $f_s=f_xx_s+f_yy_s$. Since $f_x=2x$, $f_y=-4$, $x_s=2t$, and $y_s=2st$, $$f_s=2(2st)(2t)-4(2st)=8st^2-8st=8st(t-1).$$" 13194139535653 false] [17592186167267 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059013/assets/be6afcdd0ad9ae0d7ef377c4918442a64e1186c90c25e27ec6c224d816a602fd.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y,z)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t),y=y(s,t)$ and $z=z(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂s}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\cdot \\frac{∂z}{∂s}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x,y$ and $z:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=4,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=3,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ with respect to $s,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂s} & =4s, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂s} & =1, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂s} & =8s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂s} & =(4)\\cdot 4s+(3)\\cdot 1+(-2)\\cdot 8s \\\\  & =16s+3-16s \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535653 false] [17592186167267 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069068/assets/be6afcdd0ad9ae0d7ef377c4918442a64e1186c90c25e27ec6c224d816a602fd.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y,z)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t),y=y(s,t)$ and $z=z(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂s}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\cdot \\frac{∂z}{∂s}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x,y$ and $z:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=4,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=3,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ with respect to $s,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂s} & =4s, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂s} & =1, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂s} & =8s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂s} & =(4)\\cdot 4s+(3)\\cdot 1+(-2)\\cdot 8s \\\\  & =16s+3-16s \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535653 true] [17592186337182 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059013/assets/21cec766fe72965697304ef4288ffb2b090ddfca179210165dbcfd42ec490b27.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t)$ and $y=y(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂s}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂s}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =-\\frac{1}{({e}^{st})^{2}}=-{e}^{-2st}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =-\\frac{1}{({e}^{-st})^{2}}=-{e}^{2st}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $s,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂s} & =t{e}^{st}, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂s} & =-t{e}^{-st}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂s} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂s} \\\\  & =(-{e}^{-2st})(t{e}^{st})+(-{e}^{2st})(-t{e}^{-st}) \\\\  & =t{e}^{st}-t{e}^{-st} \\\\  & =t({e}^{st}-{e}^{-st}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535653 false] [17592186337182 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069068/assets/21cec766fe72965697304ef4288ffb2b090ddfca179210165dbcfd42ec490b27.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t)$ and $y=y(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂s}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂s}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =-\\frac{1}{({e}^{st})^{2}}=-{e}^{-2st}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =-\\frac{1}{({e}^{-st})^{2}}=-{e}^{2st}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $s,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂s} & =t{e}^{st}, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂s} & =-t{e}^{-st}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂s} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂s} \\\\  & =(-{e}^{-2st})(t{e}^{st})+(-{e}^{2st})(-t{e}^{-st}) \\\\  & =t{e}^{st}-t{e}^{-st} \\\\  & =t({e}^{st}-{e}^{-st}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535653 true] [17592186359362 1017 #edb/ref 17592186359363 13194139535653 true] [17592186359362 1089 "q-94813" 13194139535653 true] [17592186359362 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535653 true] [17592186359362 1100 "Let $f(x,y,z)={e}^{x+y+z},$ where $x=s-t,$ $y={t}^{2}$ and $z=s+t.$ Calculate $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}.$" 13194139535653 true] [17592186359362 1113 #uuid "0a6b18b5-6eca-5bcb-8bc4-42eedbe5cf58" 13194139535653 true] [17592186359362 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069068/assets/b593a1b60fe8c1aef2c5c229ff426db350e6296b9ecb767494283f0fa3342072.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y,z)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t),y=y(s,t)$ and $z=z(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂t}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\cdot \\frac{∂z}{∂t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x,y$ and $z:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}={e}^{x+y+z},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}={e}^{x+y+z},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}={e}^{x+y+z}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & ={e}^{(s-t)+{t}^{2}+(s+t)}={e}^{2s+{t}^{2}}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & ={e}^{(s-t)+{t}^{2}+(s+t)}={e}^{2s+{t}^{2}}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & ={e}^{(s-t)+{t}^{2}+(s+t)}={e}^{2s+{t}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂t} & =-1, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂t} & =2t, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂t} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂t} & =({e}^{2s+{t}^{2}})\\cdot (-1)+({e}^{2s+{t}^{2}})\\cdot 2t+({e}^{2s+{t}^{2}})\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-{e}^{2s+{t}^{2}}+2t{e}^{2s+{t}^{2}}+{e}^{2s+{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =2t{e}^{2s+{t}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535653 true] [17592186359363 1000 #edb/ref 17592186359364 13194139535653 true] [17592186359363 1000 #edb/ref 17592186359365 13194139535653 true] [17592186359363 1000 #edb/ref 17592186359366 13194139535653 true] [17592186359363 1000 #edb/ref 17592186359367 13194139535653 true] [17592186359363 1000 #edb/ref 17592186359368 13194139535653 true] [17592186359363 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535653 true] [17592186359363 1041 "selection" 13194139535653 true] [17592186359363 1104 #edb/ref 17592186359368 13194139535653 true] [17592186359363 1110 #uuid "73434767-a57d-5756-8581-c4b42bcddadf" 13194139535653 true] [17592186359364 1034 "st{e}^{2s+{t}^{2}}" 13194139535653 true] [17592186359364 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535653 true] [17592186359364 1111 #uuid "4091250c-7695-5baf-ba82-311a7d20076d" 13194139535653 true] [17592186359365 1034 "(t+2){e}^{2s+{t}^{2}}" 13194139535653 true] [17592186359365 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535653 true] [17592186359365 1111 #uuid "2b89daae-b256-5c93-87cc-b28b654fad98" 13194139535653 true] [17592186359366 1034 "4{t}^{2}{e}^{2s+{t}^{2}}" 13194139535653 true] [17592186359366 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535653 true] [17592186359366 1111 #uuid "08658397-2af8-577b-be9d-b0c1d27357d7" 13194139535653 true] [17592186359367 1034 "({t}^{2}-1){e}^{2s+{t}^{2}}" 13194139535653 true] [17592186359367 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535653 true] [17592186359367 1111 #uuid "303ef2df-4798-5c6e-a1da-3514b057cdd2" 13194139535653 true] [17592186359368 1034 "2t{e}^{2s+{t}^{2}}" 13194139535653 true] [17592186359368 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535653 true] [17592186359368 1111 #uuid "d245bd8d-566f-561b-a623-f8d7a5be3694" 13194139535653 true] [17592186359369 1017 #edb/ref 17592186359370 13194139535653 true] [17592186359369 1089 "q-95114" 13194139535653 true] [17592186359369 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535653 true] [17592186359369 1100 "If $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ and $y={x}^{3},$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$" 13194139535653 true] [17592186359369 1113 #uuid "da628d4c-a325-5343-bcd6-7344b3c8f181" 13194139535653 true] [17592186359369 1145 "The chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ in the case where $y=y(x)$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{x}^{6}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}(1+{x}^{4})}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{x\\sqrt{1+{x}^{4}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{4}}}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{x}^{6}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}(1+{x}^{4})}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{x\\sqrt{1+{x}^{4}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{4}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivative $y$ with respect to $x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =3{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{4}}}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{4}}}\\cdot 3{x}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{4}}}+\\frac{3{x}^{4}}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{4}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+3{x}^{4}}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{4}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535653 true] [17592186359370 1000 #edb/ref 17592186359371 13194139535653 true] [17592186359370 1000 #edb/ref 17592186359372 13194139535653 true] [17592186359370 1000 #edb/ref 17592186359373 13194139535653 true] [17592186359370 1000 #edb/ref 17592186359374 13194139535653 true] [17592186359370 1000 #edb/ref 17592186359375 13194139535653 true] [17592186359370 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535653 true] [17592186359370 1041 "selection" 13194139535653 true] [17592186359370 1104 #edb/ref 17592186359371 13194139535653 true] [17592186359370 1110 #uuid "6e425e93-f692-5345-8f23-5bdc03925a49" 13194139535653 true] [17592186359371 1034 "\\frac{1+3{x}^{4}}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{4}}}" 13194139535653 true] [17592186359371 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535653 true] [17592186359371 1111 #uuid "67ca6dfb-d6af-559c-82bf-ebef070247b1" 13194139535653 true] [17592186359372 1034 "\\frac{3{x}^{4}}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{4}}}" 13194139535653 true] [17592186359372 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535653 true] [17592186359372 1111 #uuid "2e7e3d5f